# `const es = kit.list(source); <a branch>; for (const [k, v] of es)`: one written call is walked more than once: the join in front of it once for each literal that a way brings (tally), the turns of the counting loop written out (firstWanted).
# One walk finds Object.entries, another a function of the program that makes pairs, and what they give meets in front of the loop. A note keyed by the written call once said "flat" of whatever had met: pairs were read as keys and values: `x,1=y,2!`.
# Now the loop asks the record that made its list, and the call lists flat only where the loop's open stands right behind it in its own block: here it never does.
# f1 = byName, f2 = tally, f3 = firstWanted
